  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "The Italian alphabet has 21 letters: a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v, z. The letters j, k, w, x and y appear only in foreign words. Italian is read almost exactly as it is written, so once you know the few rules below, you can read any word.",
      "Sounds are written between slashes: /k/ is the sound at the start of *kite*.",
      {
        "h": "Step 1: hard and soft vowels"
      },
      "Italian has five vowels. *a, o, u* are hard, and *e, i* are soft. A soft vowel softens the *c*, *g* or *sc* in front of it.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Letters",
            "Before a, o, u: hard",
            "Before e, i: soft"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c",
              "/k/ as in *k*ite: casa, come, cuore",
              "/tʃ/ as in *ch*urch: cena, cinema"
            ],
            [
              "g",
              "/g/ as in *g*o: gatto, gonna, gusto",
              "/dʒ/ as in *j*am: gelato, giro"
            ],
            [
              "sc",
              "/sk/ as in *sk*y: scala, sconto, scuola",
              "/ʃ/ as in *sh*op: scena, sci"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Step 2: to keep the sound hard before e or i, add h"
      },
      "The *h* stands between the consonant and the soft vowel, so the vowel cannot soften it. The *h* has no sound of its own, here or anywhere else in Italian.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Spelling",
            "Sound",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c**h**e, c**h**i",
              "/k/",
              "che, chilo"
            ],
            [
              "g**h**e, g**h**i",
              "/g/",
              "spaghetti, laghi"
            ],
            [
              "sc**h**e, sc**h**i",
              "/sk/",
              "scherzo, schiena"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Step 3: to make the sound soft before a, o or u, add i"
      },
      "This *i* only softens the consonant and is not pronounced: *ciao* is /ˈtʃao/, not /tʃiˈao/. The only exception is when the stress falls on that *i*: then you do hear it, as in *farmacia* /farmaˈtʃia/.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Spelling",
            "Sound",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c**i**a, c**i**o, c**i**u",
              "/tʃ/",
              "ciao, cioccolato, ciuffo"
            ],
            [
              "g**i**a, g**i**o, g**i**u",
              "/dʒ/",
              "giallo, giorno, giusto"
            ],
            [
              "sc**i**a, sc**i**o, sc**i**u",
              "/ʃ/",
              "sciarpa, sciopero, sciupare"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "The whole system"
      },
      "With these three steps, each sound can go with each vowel. The underlined letters are the added *h* or *i*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "groups": [
            [
              "",
              1
            ],
            [
              "Hard vowels",
              3
            ],
            [
              "Soft vowels",
              2
            ]
          ],
          "head": [
            "Sound",
            "+ a",
            "+ o",
            "+ u",
            "+ e",
            "+ i"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/k/",
              "ca",
              "co",
              "cu",
              "c**h**e",
              "c**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/tʃ/",
              "c**i**a",
              "c**i**o",
              "c**i**u",
              "ce",
              "ci"
            ],
            [
              "/g/",
              "ga",
              "go",
              "gu",
              "g**h**e",
              "g**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/dʒ/",
              "g**i**a",
              "g**i**o",
              "g**i**u",
              "ge",
              "gi"
            ],
            [
              "/sk/",
              "sca",
              "sco",
              "scu",
              "sc**h**e",
              "sc**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/ʃ/",
              "sc**i**a",
              "sc**i**o",
              "sc**i**u",
              "sce",
              "sci"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Two more sounds"
      },
      "*gn* is always one sound, /ɲ/, close to the *ny* in *canyon*: *gnocchi, bagno*. *gli* is also one sound, /ʎ/, close to the *lli* in *million*: *figli, famiglia*. In *famiglia*, the *i* before the *a* is again only spelling. Any other *gl* sounds as written: *inglese*.",
      {
        "h": "Double consonants"
      },
      "Double consonants are held longer: *pala* (shovel) and *palla* (ball) are different words.",
      {
        "h": "Stress"
      },
      "Most words are stressed on the second-to-last syllable: **CA**-sa, a-**MI**-co. When the stress falls on the last vowel, it is written with an accent: citt**à**, caff**è**, perch**é**."
    ]
